```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the Linux network interface eth1 on
leaf_router_0_0, which is the physical link connecting that leaf
to spine_router_0_1 (the 172.16.0.8/31 subnet). The interface is
administratively down — its kernel flags show <BROADCAST,MULTICAST>
with no UP or LOWER_UP bits set — meaning it was explicitly brought
down with "ip link set eth1 down" (or an equivalent startup-script
omission). With no L2 carrier, FRR on leaf_router_0_0 cannot establish
a TCP session to 172.16.0.8; the BGP FSM moved to Active and FRR itself
logged the reset reason as "No path to specified Neighbor." The session
has been down for approximately 1 m 19 s at observation time, with the
connect-retry timer cycling every 120 s.

On the spine_router_0_1 side, BGP Graceful Restart helper mode is
masking the failure: spine_router_0_1 still reports the session as
Established and is preserving stale forwarding entries for 10.0.0.0/24
for up to 360 seconds. Once that stale-path timer expires, spine_router_0_1
will also withdraw those routes, at which point half the fabric's
redundancy to leaf_router_0_0 is permanently lost until the session
recovers.

Root cause: leaf_router_0_0's eth1 interface was administratively
shut down, breaking the data-plane link and the eBGP session to
spine_router_0_1.

Corrective fix: on leaf_router_0_0, run "ip link set eth1 up". The
interface has a valid IP address (172.16.0.9/31) already configured;
once the link comes up, BGP will reconnect to 172.16.0.8 (spine_router_0_1,
AS 65101) automatically within the next connect-retry interval, and full
dual-spine redundancy will be restored.
=== END ===
```